          "content": "Olmesartan, an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), is strongly associated with a severe form of gastrointestinal injury known as sprue-like enteropathy. First described in 2012, this condition can manifest anywhere in the GI tract, including the stomach (collagenous gastritis), small intestine (enteropathy), and colon (collagenous colitis). The clinical presentation often includes chronic diarrhea, vomiting, and significant weight loss, mimicking celiac disease. The pathophysiology is believed to be a delayed, cell-mediated immune response, not a direct toxic effect, which explains why symptoms can appear months to years after starting the medication. Histologically, it can cause villous atrophy, increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, and, characteristically, the deposition of a thick subepithelial collagen band. The diagnosis is confirmed by the complete resolution of clinical and histological abnormalities following the withdrawal of olmesartan, which is also the definitive treatment."
