            "content": "Abdominal ultrasound is the imaging modality of choice for confirming hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (HPS) due to its high sensitivity and specificity and lack of ionizing radiation. The diagnosis is made based on specific measurements of the pyloric muscle:\n1.  **Pyloric Muscle Thickness (PMT)**: A single wall thickness of **> 3 mm** is the most accurate and widely used criterion.\n2.  **Pyloric Channel Length (PCL)**: A measurement of **> 14-16 mm** is considered abnormal.\n3.  **Pyloric Diameter**: An overall pyloric diameter of > 14 mm can also be used.\n\nOther qualitative signs on ultrasound include the \"target sign\" on transverse view (hypoechoic muscle ring around echogenic mucosa) and the \"cervix sign\" on longitudinal view. The real-time observation of a non-relaxing, elongated pyloric channel with no passage of gastric contents into the duodenum is also diagnostic.",
