          "content": "The Programmed Death-1 (PD-1) pathway is a critical immune checkpoint for maintaining peripheral tolerance after allogeneic HSCT. Host tissues, particularly in inflammatory environments, upregulate the ligand PD-L1. When activated donor T-cells (which express the PD-1 receptor) engage with PD-L1 on host cells, an inhibitory signal is transmitted into the T-cell. This signal dampens the T-cell's activity, leading to a state of anergy or exhaustion, which prevents excessive tissue damage and controls GVHD. Nivolumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks the PD-1 receptor. By doing so, it prevents the inhibitory signal from being delivered to the donor T-cells. This effectively 'releases the brakes' on the alloreactive donor T-cells, leading to their unrestrained proliferation and cytotoxic attack on host tissues, thereby initiating or severely worsening acute GVHD. This same mechanism also enhances the desired anti-tumor effect (Graft-versus-Lymphoma effect), as the donor T-cells are also more effective at attacking malignant cells."
