            "content": "Ulcerative keratitis, or a corneal ulcer, is an open sore on the cornea. On slit-lamp examination, the cardinal signs include:\n1.  **Epithelial Defect**: A discontinuation in the corneal epithelium, which is the hallmark of an ulcer. This area vividly stains bright green with the application of fluorescein dye under cobalt blue light.\n2.  **Stromal Infiltrate**: An opacification within the corneal stroma, underlying the epithelial defect. This opacity consists of inflammatory cells and cellular debris and appears as a hazy, gray, or white area.\n3.  **Anterior Chamber Reaction**: Inflammation can extend into the anterior chamber, causing the appearance of white blood cells (cells) and protein (flare) in the aqueous humor. In severe cases, a layer of white blood cells can settle inferiorly, a condition known as a hypopyon.\n4.  **Associated Signs**: These include conjunctival injection (redness, especially around the cornea, known as ciliary flush), eyelid swelling (edema), pain, photophobia, and discharge."
