            "content": "Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by mutations in the CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator) gene. The CFTR protein is an ion channel responsible for transporting chloride and bicarbonate ions across epithelial cell membranes. In the fetal intestine, dysfunctional CFTR channels lead to decreased secretion of chloride, bicarbonate, and consequently water into the intestinal lumen. This results in dehydration of the intraluminal contents. Additionally, in most CF patients, thick secretions block the pancreatic ducts (pancreatic insufficiency), preventing digestive enzymes from reaching the gut. The combination of reduced water content and undigested proteins/macromolecules leads to the formation of abnormally viscous, sticky, and inspissated meconium. This thick meconium becomes impacted, typically in the terminal ileum, causing a mechanical obstruction known as meconium ileus."
