            "knowledge": "In sarcoidosis, hypercalcemia is not driven by the parathyroid glands. Instead, it results from the unregulated production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active form of vitamin D, by activated macrophages within the sarcoid granulomas. These macrophages possess the enzyme 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is normally found in the kidneys and regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH). In sarcoidosis, this enzyme activity is autonomous and not subject to normal physiological feedback. The resulting excess calcitriol leads to increased intestinal absorption of calcium and enhanced bone resorption, causing serum calcium levels to rise. Consequently, the elevated calcium suppresses the secretion of PTH from the parathyroid glands.",
