            "topic": "Key histopathological differences between sarcoidosis and infectious granulomatous diseases.",
            "knowledge": "The primary histopathological distinction lies in the presence or absence of central necrosis within the granulomas. Sarcoidosis is characterized by the formation of well-demarcated, compact, non-necrotising (or non-caseating) epithelioid granulomas. These consist of tight clusters of epithelioid histiocytes and multinucleated giant cells, often with a scant peripheral rim of lymphocytes. In contrast, infectious diseases like tuberculosis classically produce caseating granulomas, which feature a central zone of amorphous, eosinophilic, necrotic debris (caseous necrosis). Fungal infections can also cause caseating granulomas. Definitive diagnosis of an infectious cause requires identification of the organism using special stains (e.g., Acid-Fast Bacilli stain for mycobacteria, Gomori methenamine silver for fungi) or culture, which are negative in sarcoidosis.",
            "relevance_to_diagnosis": "This is a cornerstone of the diagnosis. The case report explicitly states that the histopathological examination of the skin biopsy showed 'non-necrotising granulomas'. This finding is the hallmark of sarcoidosis and provides strong evidence against tuberculosis or a fungal infection, which are the main differential diagnoses for a systemic granulomatous disease. It solidifies the diagnosis of an inflammatory, non-infectious etiology."
