            "content": "In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease), the adrenal glands are damaged and cannot produce sufficient cortisol. This lack of cortisol eliminates the negative feedback signal to the hypothalamus and pituitary gland. Consequently, the pituitary gland continuously secretes high levels of its precursor hormone, pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC). POMC is cleaved to produce several active hormones, including adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) and melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH). Both ACTH and MSH can bind to the melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) on melanocytes in the skin and mucous membranes. This binding stimulates melanin production, leading to the characteristic generalized hyperpigmentation. The darkening is often most pronounced in sun-exposed areas, skin creases (e.g., palmar creases), pressure points (knuckles, elbows), recent scars, and mucosal surfaces like the gums and buccal mucosa. This sign is specific to primary adrenal insufficiency because in secondary adrenal insufficiency (due to pituitary failure), ACTH levels are low, and therefore hyperpigmentation does not occur.",
