            "content": "Frey's syndrome, also known as auriculotemporal syndrome, is a neurological condition caused by damage to the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve. This nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that stimulate the parotid gland to produce saliva, as well as sympathetic fibers that control sweating (sudomotor) and blood vessel diameter (vasomotor) in the overlying skin. When the nerve is injured—for instance, by the pressure or trauma from an obstetric forceps blade during delivery—the fibers can regenerate aberrantly. During this 'miswiring,' the regenerating parasympathetic (salivatory) fibers mistakenly innervate the cutaneous blood vessels and sweat glands that were previously supplied by sympathetic fibers. As a result, when a gustatory stimulus (like eating) triggers a signal for salivation, this signal is misdirected, causing localized vasodilation (flushing) and/or sweating in the skin supplied by the nerve.",
