            "topic": "Pathophysiology of hyperpigmentation in primary adrenal insufficiency.",
            "knowledge": "In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease), the adrenal glands fail to produce cortisol. The hypothalamus and pituitary gland sense this low cortisol level and, due to the loss of negative feedback, the pituitary gland dramatically increases its secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) in an attempt to stimulate the failing adrenal glands. ACTH is synthesized from a large precursor molecule called pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC). When POMC is cleaved to produce ACTH, other peptides, including α-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH), are also produced in excess. Both ACTH and α-MSH can bind to the melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) on melanocytes in the skin and mucous membranes. This binding stimulates the production and deposition of melanin, leading to a generalized darkening (hyperpigmentation) of the skin. The effect is most pronounced in sun-exposed areas, skin creases (like palmar creases), pressure points (knuckles, elbows), recent scars, and mucosal surfaces such as the gingiva, buccal mucosa, and tongue.",
