        },
        {
            "topic": "Mechanism of electrolyte disturbances (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia) in mineralocorticoid deficiency.",
            "knowledge": "Primary adrenal insufficiency involves the destruction of the entire adrenal cortex, leading to a deficiency of not only glucocorticoids (cortisol) but also mineralocorticoids, primarily aldosterone. Aldosterone's main role is to regulate salt and water balance by acting on the distal tubules and collecting ducts of the kidneys. It promotes the reabsorption of sodium (and water) from the urine back into the blood and simultaneously promotes the excretion of potassium into the urine. In aldosterone deficiency, this process is impaired. The kidneys cannot effectively retain sodium, leading to urinary sodium wasting, volume depletion, hypotension, and hyponatremia (low blood sodium). Concurrently, potassium excretion is reduced, causing potassium to be retained in the blood, resulting in hyperkalemia (high blood potassium).",
            "diagnostic_help": "This pathophysiology perfectly explains the patient's life-threatening electrolyte abnormalities. His laboratory results showed a sodium concentration of 116 mmol/L (severe hyponatremia) and a potassium concentration of 5.3 mmol/L (hyperkalemia). These findings, in conjunction with his dehydration and severe postural hypotension, are the direct consequence of aldosterone deficiency, confirming that the entire adrenal cortex is failing. This provides definitive biochemical evidence for primary adrenal insufficiency."
