            "knowledge": "While the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in developed countries is autoimmune disease (often marked by anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies), the differential diagnosis in a boy or young man must include X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD). X-ALD is a genetic disorder caused by mutations in the ABCD1 gene, leading to the accumulation of very long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA) in various tissues, including the adrenal cortex and the white matter of the brain. This accumulation is toxic and leads to adrenal cell destruction and demyelination. Therefore, patients with X-ALD can present with adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease), a progressive neurological disorder (myelopathy or cerebral ALD), or both. The diagnosis is confirmed by measuring elevated levels of VLCFA in the blood and by genetic testing for ABCD1 mutations.",
