            "content": "Acanthocytes (spur cells) are red blood cells with a few, irregularly spaced, spiky projections of varying length and width. They are a hallmark of severe liver disease (spur cell hemolytic anemia). The formation of acanthocytes is due to altered plasma lipid composition, specifically an increased cholesterol-to-phospholipid ratio, which leads to the abnormal deposition of cholesterol into the red cell membrane. While the defining characteristic is the spiky shape, these cells typically exist within a background of other morphological changes caused by liver disease. Sources indicate that acanthocytes themselves are often normocytic or only slightly larger than normal, but they arise in a setting where generalized macrocytosis is common. The underlying pathology that creates acanthocytes also increases the overall red cell surface area, contributing to a larger cell size.",
