            "content": "Antimitochondrial antibodies (AMA) are a group of autoantibodies directed against components of the inner mitochondrial membrane. While highly associated with Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC), the standard immunofluorescence test for AMA is not entirely specific. There are nine subtypes of mitochondrial autoantigens (M1-M9). The AMA seen in PBC is almost exclusively directed against the M2 subtype (the E2 component of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex) and is highly specific for the disease. However, other conditions can produce antibodies against different mitochondrial antigens. Syphilis, caused by *Treponema pallidum*, induces the production of anti-cardiolipin antibodies. Cardiolipin is a phospholipid component of the inner mitochondrial membrane and is also designated as the M1 mitochondrial antigen. Therefore, the anti-cardiolipin antibodies in syphilis can cross-react in AMA immunofluorescence assays, leading to a positive result. This is considered a 'false positive' in the context of diagnosing PBC. This can be resolved by using a more specific M2 ELISA test, which would be negative in a patient with syphilis-induced AMA."
