            "content": "The pathogenesis of psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis is predominantly driven by the IL-23/Th17 axis. The pathway is initiated when dendritic cells and macrophages, upon activation, secrete interleukin-23 (IL-23). IL-23 is a heterodimeric cytokine composed of a unique p19 subunit and a p40 subunit (shared with IL-12). IL-23 binds to its receptor on T helper 17 (Th17) cells and other immune cells, promoting their survival, proliferation, and pathogenic function. The primary role of these activated Th17 cells is the production and secretion of a suite of pro-inflammatory cytokines, most notably IL-17A. IL-17A is the key downstream effector molecule that directly orchestrates the psoriatic phenotype. It acts on keratinocytes to induce hyperproliferation (acanthosis), aberrant differentiation, and the production of chemokines and antimicrobial peptides that recruit neutrophils. This leads to the characteristic erythematous, scaly plaques. In the joints, IL-17A promotes inflammation of the synovium and entheses and stimulates osteoclastogenesis, contributing to the erosive arthritis seen in psoriatic arthritis."
