          "content": "Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi, the causative agent of Typhoid fever, has a unique ability to establish a long-term, asymptomatic carrier state within the human gallbladder. This occurs in 1-5% of individuals following an acute infection. The gallbladder provides a favorable environment where the bacteria can evade the host's immune system. A crucial mechanism for this persistence is the formation of biofilms, which are structured communities of bacteria encased in a self-produced matrix of extracellular polymeric substances. S. Typhi preferentially forms these biofilms on the surface of gallstones or on the gallbladder's mucosal epithelium. The biofilm matrix protects the bacteria from both host immune cells and high concentrations of bile and antibiotics. This chronic colonization leads to persistent, low-level inflammation of the gallbladder (cholecystitis) and is a major risk factor for the development of gallbladder carcinoma. Individuals in this carrier state continuously shed bacteria in their feces, acting as a reservoir for transmitting the disease to others, as famously exemplified by Mary Mallon.",
