            "content": "The primary differential diagnosis for a swollen, red digit in an infant includes cellulitis, dactylitis, and trauma. \n- **Cellulitis:** An infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissue. It presents as diffuse erythema, warmth, and swelling, but crucially lacks a well-demarcated circumferential constricting band. The swelling in cellulitis is not typically confined distal to a sharp line.\n- **Dactylitis ('Sausage Digit'):** Inflammation of an entire digit. Causes in infants include sickle cell disease, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, or infection (e.g., blistering distal dactylitis). Dactylitis involves uniform swelling of the whole digit without a focal constriction band. \n- **Non-accidental Trauma:** A deliberately applied ligature would present identically to an accidental hair tourniquet. The physical finding is the same, but the etiology differs. HTS is the specific term for this presentation, regardless of intent, though most cases are accidental.",
