            "topic": "Pathophysiology of purine metabolism, specifically the role of xanthine dehydrogenase in converting xanthine to uric acid.",
            "content": "Purine metabolism involves the breakdown of purine nucleotides (from dietary sources or cellular turnover) into uric acid for excretion. The final two steps of this pathway are catalyzed by a single enzyme, xanthine dehydrogenase/oxidase (XDH/XO). First, XDH/XO converts hypoxanthine to xanthine. Second, it converts xanthine to uric acid. In hereditary xanthinuria, a genetic mutation in the XDH gene (Type I) or a related gene necessary for its function (Type II) leads to a non-functional or absent enzyme. This creates a metabolic block. Consequently, the precursors hypoxanthine and xanthine cannot be converted to the final product, uric acid. This leads to: 1) Accumulation of xanthine and hypoxanthine in the blood and tissues. 2) Drastically reduced production of uric acid, resulting in hypouricemia and hypouricosuria. Because xanthine is poorly soluble in urine, its high concentration can lead to precipitation, forming crystals and stones (urolithiasis), which is the primary cause of symptoms."
        }
