            "content": "Post-herpetic abdominal pseudohernia is a rare complication of herpes zoster, caused by zoster paresis of the abdominal wall muscles (e.g., external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis). It typically involves the T7-T12 nerve roots. The key clinical feature is a unilateral bulge or laxity of the abdominal wall that becomes more prominent upon standing or with increased intra-abdominal pressure (like coughing). Unlike a true hernia, there is no underlying fascial defect, so there is no risk of incarceration. The paresis can occur before (zoster sine herpete paresis), during, or after the appearance of the herpetic rash. The prognosis is generally favorable, with most patients experiencing partial to complete recovery of muscle function over several months.",
