            "content": "Lead is a heavy metal that interferes with multiple enzyme systems in the body. Hematologically, it has a profound effect on heme synthesis. Lead inhibits two key enzymes in this pathway: delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD) and ferrochelatase. The inhibition of ferrochelatase is particularly critical, as this enzyme is responsible for the final step of incorporating iron into the protoporphyrin IX ring to form heme. The resulting impairment in heme production leads to a microcytic, hypochromic anemia. Furthermore, lead is radiopaque, meaning it absorbs X-rays and appears as bright white densities on a radiograph. When ingested, lead-containing particles (like paint chips) can be visualized in the gastrointestinal tract."
        },
