            "content": "Leukoplakia is a clinical term for a white patch, and its histopathology is defined by hyperkeratosis (thickening of the keratin layer) with or without acanthosis (thickening of the spinous cell layer). The most critical histopathological finding in leukoplakia is the presence or absence of epithelial dysplasia, which represents disordered cell growth and is a precursor to carcinoma. Dysplasia is characterized by features such as nuclear hyperchromatism, pleomorphism, loss of basal cell polarity, and increased mitotic figures. The underlying connective tissue typically shows a mild, chronic, non-specific inflammatory infiltrate. \n\nPlaque-type Oral Lichen Planus (OLP), conversely, has more specific features. Histologically, it is characterized by a dense, band-like infiltrate of T-lymphocytes in the lamina propria that is immediately adjacent to the epithelium. A key feature is hydropic (liquefaction) degeneration of the basal keratinocytes. Saw-tooth-shaped rete pegs and colloid (Civatte) bodies may also be present. While plaque-type OLP shows hyperkeratosis, the distinctive basal cell damage and the specific nature of the inflammatory infiltrate differentiate it from leukoplakia."
        },
