            "content": "Chronic Hyperplastic Candidiasis (CHC), also known as candidal leukoplakia, is a distinct form of oral candidiasis that presents as a persistent, non-scrapable white plaque. Clinically, it can be indistinguishable from other forms of leukoplakia. It is most commonly found on the buccal mucosa, near the commissures. \n\nThe definitive diagnosis is made through biopsy and histopathology. The microscopic features include epithelial hyperplasia (parakeratosis and acanthosis) similar to leukoplakia, but with the crucial finding of Candida fungal hyphae invading the superficial layers of the epithelium. These hyphae are best visualized with special stains like Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) or Grocott methenamine silver (GMS).\n\nCHC is considered a specific subtype of leukoplakia. There is evidence that the chronic Candida infection can induce epithelial changes, and CHC has been shown to have a higher rate of epithelial dysplasia and malignant transformation than candidiasis-free leukoplakia. Some cases may resolve after intensive antifungal therapy, but a biopsy remains essential to assess for dysplasia, as the presence of Candida does not preclude malignant potential."
